    {
      "page_title": "One Thread, Blocking & The Threadpool",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. One Thread Only",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Node runs your JS on ONE thread, one call stack.",
            "Every synchronous line blocks every other request.",
            "A 2s `for` loop = 2s of dead server, for everyone."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Blocking vs Non-Blocking",
          "items": [
            "Blocking: returns only when work is done.",
            "`fs.readFileSync(p)` — the stack waits.",
            "Non-blocking: returns now, result delivered later.",
            "`fs.readFile(p, cb)` — the loop keeps going."
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. I/O-Bound vs CPU-Bound",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Async fixes WAITING on I/O, not CPU work.",
            "Hashing, parsing, image resize stay blocking.",
            "Concurrency = interleaved in-flight ops, one thread.",
            "Parallelism needs `worker_threads` / `child_process`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "app.get('/hash', (req, res) => {",
            "  res.end(bcrypt.hashSync(pw, 12)); // serial!",
            "});",
            "// bcrypt.hash(pw, 12) -> threadpool, concurrent"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Runtime Is Not Single-Threaded",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Work",
            "Handled by",
            "Pool slot?"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "fs, zlib",
              "libuv pool",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "dns.lookup",
              "libuv pool",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "pbkdf2, scrypt",
              "libuv pool",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "sockets, HTTP",
              "epoll/kqueue/IOCP",
              "no"
            ],
            [
              "pool size",
              "UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE",
              "4 default"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Rule of thumb",
          "text": "Never do more than ~10ms of synchronous work in a request path. Past that, chunk it, stream it, or move it off-thread."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Find the blocker: `node --cpu-prof`, `node --prof`.",
            "Add an event-loop-lag probe as a health metric."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Phase",
            "Runs"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "timers",
              "setTimeout / setInterval"
            ],
            [
              "poll",
              "I/O cbs; blocks here when idle"
            ],
            [
              "check",
              "setImmediate — always after poll"
            ],
            [
              "close",
              "socket.on('close')"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Six Loop Phases & Microtask Order",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Style Timeline",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "callbacks 2009",
              "promises ES2015 / Node 4",
              "async-await Node 7.6",
              "unflagged Node 8"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "p.then(x => { doAsync() })  // undefined, no wait",
            "p.then(x => doAsync())      // returns promise",
            "p.then(x => { return doAsync() })  // also fine"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Loop Phases In Order",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "timers",
              "pending cbs",
              "idle/prepare",
              "poll",
              "check",
              "close"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Timers are a floor, never a guarantee.",
            "Microtasks drain BETWEEN every macrotask callback.",
            "Order: `process.nextTick` queue, then promises.",
            "Recursive nextTick starves the loop; setImmediate can't."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Memorise this puzzle",
          "text": "At top level, `setTimeout(fn,0)` vs `setImmediate` is NON-deterministic (depends on loop startup). Inside an I/O callback, `setImmediate` always wins."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Trace The Order",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "log('1'); log('2');",
            "process.nextTick(() => log('nextTick'));",
            "Promise.resolve().then(() => log('promise'));",
            "setTimeout(() => log('timeout'), 0);",
            "setImmediate(() => log('immediate'));"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Prints: 1 2 nextTick promise timeout immediate.",
            "Sync code first — it owns the stack until it returns.",
            "Each phase drains up to a system max, then moves on.",
            "So one phase cannot indefinitely starve the next."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Error-First Contract",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "fs.readFile('a.txt','utf8', (err, data) => {",
            "  if (err) return cb(err);   // return!",
            "  cb(null, data.trim());     // null on success",
            "});"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Measuring Event-Loop Lag",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Loop Lag",
          "items": [
            "Lag = should-have-fired minus did-fire.",
            "`perf_hooks.monitorEventLoopDelay()`.",
            "Browsers have no nextTick — demos don't port 1:1."
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Measure The Lag",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const h = monitorEventLoopDelay(",
            "  { resolution: 20 });",
            "h.enable();",
            "// later, report as health metric:",
            "h.percentile(99) / 1e6; // ns -> ms"
          ],
          "note": "histogram values are in nanoseconds"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Alert on p99, not mean",
          "text": "A healthy loop sits near the resolution. A rising ==p99 lag== means something **synchronous** is hogging the thread — profile with `--cpu-prof`."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Error-First Rules, Zalgo & Promisify",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Error is ALWAYS the first parameter, `null` on success.",
            "Miss the `return` and the callback fires twice.",
            "The runtime invokes your callback, not your code.",
            "So `try/catch` around the call cannot catch inside it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Throw = death",
          "text": "A throw inside a callback with no handler becomes an `uncaughtException` and kills the process."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Zalgo & Once",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "// never sometimes-sync, sometimes-async",
            "if (hit) return process.nextTick(",
            "  () => cb(null, hit));   // defer the cache path",
            "fs.readFile(k, cb);"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Call the callback exactly once — no more, no less.",
            "Guard with `let called = false` or `once()`.",
            "Matters most when wrapping event-based APIs."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. The Promisify Bridge",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Tool",
            "Direction",
            "Note"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "util.promisify",
              "cb → promise",
              "err-first only"
            ],
            [
              "util.callbackify",
              "promise → cb",
              "reverse path"
            ],
            [
              "promisify.custom",
              "library-defined",
              "custom shape"
            ],
            [
              "cb(null,a,b)",
              "lossy",
              "only `a` survives"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Core still ships callback APIs everywhere.",
            "Promise mirrors: `fs/promises`, `dns/promises`.",
            "`timers/promises` gives a promise `setTimeout`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Pyramid Of Doom",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "readConfig((e, c) => {",
            "  connectDb(c, (e, db) => {",
            "    db.query(q, (e, u) => {",
            "      writeLog(u, (e) => done(e));  // 4 deep",
            "    }); }); });"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Growing rightward",
          "text": "Every level repeats `if (err) return cb(err)`, and indentation encodes control flow. Nesting is the symptom; the disease is on the next page."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Settle vs fire",
          "text": "A promise settles ONCE. A listener fires many times. Don't promisify a stream of events."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Transitions are one-way and irreversible.",
            "Settled = fulfilled or rejected.",
            "A promise is an OBJECT for a future value.",
            "So it can be returned, stored, passed — a callback can't."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Why Callbacks Failed & Promise States",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Three Structural Failures",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Errors don't compose — every level forwards by hand.",
            "One missed forward silently swallows the failure.",
            "No return value — you can't build pipelines.",
            "Control flow is manual: parallel needs your own latch."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "let n = 3, out = [];",
            "const done = i => (e, v) => {",
            "  out[i] = v;",
            "  if (--n === 0) final(null, out);  // the latch",
            "};"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. What People Tried",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Fix",
            "Gives you"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "named functions",
              "flatter nesting"
            ],
            [
              "async.series",
              "ordered steps"
            ],
            [
              "async.parallel",
              "fan-out + join"
            ],
            [
              "async.waterfall",
              "value passing"
            ],
            [
              "async.queue(w, n)",
              "first concurrency limiter"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`async.queue` is the ancestor of today's `p-limit`.",
            "Callbacks + `try/catch` don't mix; + `throw` don't mix.",
            "That is the structural reason promises won.",
            "Still right for repeated events: EventEmitter listeners."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Promise States",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "pending",
              "fulfilled (value) | rejected (reason)",
              "settled — permanent"
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Promise Mechanics & The Flat Chain",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Wrapping Legacy APIs",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const read = p => new Promise((res, rej) =>",
            "  fs.readFile(p, (e, d) => {",
            "    if (e) return rej(e);",
            "    res(d);        // 2nd resolve = no-op",
            "  }));"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Idempotence kills the double-callback bug class.",
            "`Promise.resolve(v)` / `reject(e)` = already settled.",
            "`Promise.resolve(p)` on a promise returns it unchanged."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. then / catch / finally",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call",
            "Same as",
            "Returns"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              ".then(f, r)",
              "—",
              "new promise"
            ],
            [
              ".catch(f)",
              ".then(undefined, f)",
              "new promise"
            ],
            [
              ".finally(f)",
              "both paths",
              "value passes through"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`.then` ALWAYS returns a new promise — that's chaining.",
            "Handlers run as microtasks, never synchronously.",
            "`Promise.resolve(1).then(log)` logs after all sync code.",
            "Returning a thenable adopts its state — flattens, not nests."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Two Promise Traps",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "// anti-pattern: wrapping a promise",
            "new Promise(res => doAsync().then(res));",
            "// just:",
            "return doAsync();"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`throw` inside `.then` rejects the returned promise.",
            "So `throw e` and `reject(e)` are equivalent there.",
            "Promises are EAGER — the executor runs at construction.",
            "No built-in laziness, no built-in cancel."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Flat Chain",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "readConfig()",
            "  .then(connect)",
            "  .then(query)",
            "  .then(write)",
            "  .catch(handle);   // always terminate"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Most common chaining bug",
          "text": "Not returning the inner promise. The chain moves on before the work finishes, and errors escape the `.catch`."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Error Propagation & Promise Combinators",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Errors Flow Downhill",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A rejection skips every `.then` until the first `.catch`.",
            "`.catch` returning a value RESUMES in fulfilled state.",
            "To keep failing, rethrow inside the `.catch`.",
            "Unterminated chain = unhandled rejection."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Placement",
            "Catches errors from `a`?"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              ".then(a).catch(b)",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              ".then(a, b)",
              "no — upstream only"
            ],
            [
              "chain returned",
              "caller's problem"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Fork vs Sequence",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Two `.then` on the SAME promise = two independent chains.",
            "That branches; it does not sequence.",
            "Passing values down needs closure nesting or an accumulator.",
            "That ergonomic weakness is what async/await removes."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "c.query(sql)",
            " .then(use)",
            " .catch(log)",
            " .finally(() => c.release());  // both paths"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Combinators Open",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call",
            "Settles when",
            "Order"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "all",
              "first rejection / all done",
              "INPUT order"
            ],
            [
              "allSettled",
              "all settled, never rejects",
              "INPUT order"
            ],
            [
              "race",
              "first to settle, either way",
              "n/a"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "allSettled yields {status,value} or {status,reason}.",
            "Use it when partial failure is acceptable.",
            "`race` is the basis of the classic timeout pattern."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "all fails fast, cancels nothing",
          "text": "The other operations keep running after the first rejection — and their later rejections can surface as unhandled."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. any & AggregateError",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const first = await Promise.any([a(), b()]);",
            "// Node 15+: first FULFILLMENT wins",
            "// all reject -> AggregateError",
            "// e.errors holds every reason"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Combinator Picks & async/await Basics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Pick",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "all",
              "every-or-nothing"
            ],
            [
              "allSettled",
              "report card"
            ],
            [
              "race",
              "first settled"
            ],
            [
              "any",
              "first success"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Fan Out, Then Partition",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const rs = await Promise.allSettled(jobs);",
            "const ok = rs.filter(r => r.status === 'fulfilled');",
            "const bad = rs.filter(r => r.status === 'rejected');  // retry"
          ],
          "note": "test status — a value can be undefined"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Parallel START creates concurrency, not `await`.",
            "`await Promise.all([getA(), getB()])` — calls made first.",
            "Any iterable works; non-promises get `Promise.resolve`d.",
            "10,000 items = 10,000 sockets. Combinators have NO limit."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. async Returns A Promise",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`return 1` fulfills with 1; `throw e` rejects with e.",
            "`await p` suspends the function, resumes in a microtask.",
            "It never blocks the loop or the thread.",
            "`await 1` still costs one microtask tick — not free."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "async function f() {",
            "  const a = await g();",
            "  return a + 1;",
            "}",
            "// desugars to: g().then(a => a + 1)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. try/catch Comes Back",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "try { await q(sql); }",
            "catch (e) { log(e); }",
            "finally { client.release(); }  // always"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A rejected `await` throws at that line — `catch` sees it.",
            "`finally` runs on success, error, and early `return`.",
            "No `await` inside `try` = nothing to catch."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "async function load() {",
            "  try { return await q(); }",
            "  catch (e) { return null; }",
            "}"
          ],
          "note": "plain `return q()` skips the catch"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Need",
            "Chain",
            "async/await"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "catch",
              "`.catch(fn)`",
              "`try/catch`"
            ],
            [
              "cleanup",
              "`.finally(fn)`",
              "`finally {}`"
            ],
            [
              "pass values",
              "closures",
              "plain `const`"
            ],
            [
              "recover",
              "return in catch",
              "assign in catch"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Async Iteration & The Serial Trap",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Same machinery",
          "text": "async/await is ==syntax over promises== — every rule about rejection, microtasks and combinators still applies."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Intermediate values become plain `const`s — no pyramid.",
            "That readability win is the whole point of the syntax.",
            "Top-level `await`: ESM only (.mjs / type: module).",
            "CommonJS still needs an async IIFE."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Iteration Rules",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`forEach(async …)` is fire-and-forget — return ignored.",
            "Use `for...of` (serial) or `map` + `Promise.all`.",
            "`for await (const c of stream)` walks async iterables.",
            "Idiomatic for streams and paginated APIs."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "async function* pages(url) {",
            "  let next = url;",
            "  while (next) { const p = await get(next);",
            "    yield p.items; next = p.next; }",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Stack traces",
          "text": "async/await preserves async frames far better than raw `.then` chains — `--async-stack-traces` is on by default."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Serial Trap",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const a = await getA();   // t(A)",
            "const b = await getB();   // + t(B)  serial!",
            "const [x, y] = await Promise.all([",
